<script>
import { url } from '@abide/abide'
import Doc from '#src/lib/Doc.abide'
import Example from '#src/lib/Example.abide'
import Section from '#src/lib/Section.abide'
import Contact from './lib/Contact.abide'
import contactSource from './lib/Contact.abide' with { type: 'text' }
</script>

<Doc>
    <Section title="What a name means, by position">
        <table>
            <thead><tr><th>You write</th><th>You get</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><td><code>foo</code> anywhere its position is not typed as a <code>Reactive</code></td><td>a live read</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>const x: Reactive&lt;…&gt; = foo</code> / a <code>Reactive&lt;…&gt;</code>-typed parameter or <code>bind:</code></td><td>the <code>Reactive</code> itself</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>foo = bar</code></td><td>a write, of a settled value, a load or a <code>Reactive</code> to adopt</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>foo.bar</code></td><td>the value's <code>bar</code></td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>foo.bar</code> / <code>foo.bar(…)</code> where <code>bar</code> is one of its members</td><td>the <code>Reactive</code> API</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>foo()</code> / <code>foo.set(v)</code></td><td>the two explicit forms</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>await foo</code></td><td>the value, once it has settled</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>refresh(foo)</code> / a <code>Selection</code>-typed parameter</td><td>the <code>Reactive</code> itself</td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>

        <p>The rule is <strong>position</strong>, not decoration. An operand is a place a value
        belongs, so the name reads; a position whose declared type is a <code>Reactive</code> — an
        annotated binding, a parameter, a <code>bind:</code> operand — is a place the
        <code>Reactive</code> belongs, so the name holds. That split is why a value can be passed to
        a component without being read first, and why reading one never needs a sigil to mark
        it.</p>

        <p>A <code>.ts</code> file has no sugar and writes the members out. The same explicit form
        inside a <code>.abide</code> file means exactly this, so moving code between the two changes
        what you type and never what it does.</p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="Reading is live, holding is not">
        <p><code>{'{total}'}</code> re-reads whenever <code>total</code> produces. <code>const held
        = total</code> reads too, like any other position with no type to say otherwise.
        <strong>Holding is declared in the type</strong>: <code>const held: Reactive&lt;number&gt; =
        total</code> takes the <code>Reactive</code>, which is also what a <code>bind:</code>
        operand hands over. Destructuring and spreading read once: <code>const [first, second] =
        rows</code> takes two rows, not two pieces of a <code>Reactive</code>.</p>

        <p>A name on the right of <code>=</code> is read like any operand. A <code>Reactive</code>
        that arrives any other way is <strong>adopted</strong>:
        <code>{'paid = payInvoice({ id })'}</code> makes <code>paid</code> serve what the call
        serves, and go on following it as it produces — the same thing a body returning a
        <code>Reactive</code> does. From then on a settled value written to <code>paid</code> goes
        through to what it adopted (11.59), and only another <code>Reactive</code> re-points it
        (11.61).</p>

        <p><code>await foo</code> is the one other position that holds. Every <code>Reactive</code>
        is a promise for its own settling, so <code>await invoice</code> waits for the value and
        hands it to you — the same line you would write in a <code>.ts</code> file, meaning the same
        thing. Lowered as a read it would be <code>await invoice()</code>, which takes what has
        landed and throws where nothing has, and that is never what an <code>await</code> was asking
        for.</p>

        <p><code>refresh(foo)</code> holds for the same kind of reason. <code>refresh</code>,
        <code>invalidate</code>, <code>busy</code> and <code>ready</code> take a
        <code>Selection</code> — the thing to reload or ask about — and a value is never a selection
        of itself, so <code>refresh(invoice)</code> hands over <code>invoice</code> rather than the
        invoice it holds.</p>

        <p><strong>Where you can write one</strong> is anywhere there is a function to write it in:
        a handler, a <code>reactive</code> body, an <code>async</code> arrow of your own. Not at the
        top of a <code>&lt;script&gt;</code>, and not in a template position. Setup is a function
        body and a slot is compiled into one, so neither has anything to await in, and both are
        compile errors rather than a module that will not parse. Nothing in abide holds a render, so
        there is no position where waiting would mean the page waits.</p>

        <p>Read on: <a href={url('/values/show-a-value-that-changes')}>Local state</a> ·
        <a href={url('/values/show-a-value-that-isnt-there-yet')}>Loading states</a> ·
        <a href={url('/templates/reuse-a-piece-of-markup')}>Components</a></p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="A write through a member path copies down that path">
        <Example
            id="name-path-write"
            title="`contact.address.city = v` — a write through a path"
            summary="A contact's city, two levels down. Type into the field and watch the line above it."
            files={{
                'lib/Contact.abide': contactSource,
            }}
        >
            <Contact />
        </Example>

        <p><em>1 line, 2 copies</em> — and the arm has to make both by hand, one per segment of the
        path.</p>

        <p><code>contact.address.city = 'Oslo'</code> copies <code>contact</code>, copies
        <code>contact.address</code>, sets <code>city</code>, and writes the new outer object back.
        The copy is <strong>not optimized away</strong>, even where nothing appears to be holding
        the old one. What holds it is the identity check every reader downstream is made of, and a
        value written in place is the object it already was — so nothing has a reason to re-read
        it.</p>

        <p><code>contact.tags.push('vip')</code> is the same write reached through a mutator. Which
        mutators do it is resolved from the <strong>type</strong> of the <code>Reactive</code>
        rather than from the method name, so a <code>push</code> on your own object with a
        <code>push</code> of its own is your method and not a write.</p>

        <p>Read on: <a href={url('/values/patch-a-list-from-a-live-feed')}>Patching a list</a></p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="A property access is a read">
        <p><code>{'{contact.address.city}'}</code> is exactly <code>contact().address.city</code>.
        On a value still loading it throws <code>NotLanded</code> like any read, so the hole
        <strong>renders nothing until the value lands</strong> and then fills — and where it has
        rendered before, it keeps what it showed rather than blanking. A fallback for the wait
        belongs to the probe, not to <code>??</code>:
        <code>{'{contact.ready() ? contact.name : \'—\'}'}</code>. The compiler warns on a
        <code>??</code> whose left side could only be missing because nothing has landed yet.</p>

        <p>A member of the <code>Reactive</code> itself reaches the <code>Reactive</code> rather
        than the value — <code>contact.busy()</code>, <code>contact.error()</code>, and
        <code>{'onclick={person.refresh}'}</code> without the call, which hands over
        <code>refresh</code> bound to <code>person</code>. Bound means arguments pass through: a
        keyed value's <code>refresh</code> takes a pattern, so handed straight to
        <code>onclick</code> it gets the click's event as one — wrap it in an arrow there. Anything
        else is the value's own. Where the value declares a member by the same name the compiler
        warns, and <code>contact().error</code> is how to reach the value's.</p>

        <p>A read spelled <code>foo()</code> takes no arguments, and passing one is a compile error.
        <code>foo</code> is not a function you call; <code>foo()</code> is the explicit spelling of
        a read, and an argument in it is somebody meaning something else.</p>

        <p>Read on: <a href={url('/values/show-a-value-that-isnt-there-yet')}>Loading states</a> ·
        <a href={url('/reference/reactive')}><code>reactive</code></a></p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="Next">
        <ul>
            <li><a href={url('/templates/put-a-value-in-the-markup')}>Expressions</a> — where a read
            can go in the markup</li>
            <li><a href={url('/templates/bind-a-form-to-state')}>Form binding</a> — the binding that
            reads and writes in one place</li>
            <li><a href={url('/values/show-a-value-that-changes')}>Local state</a> — what
            <code>reactive</code> hands back to name</li>
        </ul>
    </Section>
</Doc>
